# Error Handling Guidelines

## Core Principles

Helicone uses a consistent result-based error handling pattern across the codebase to avoid throwing exceptions and to make error states explicit.

## Result Type Pattern

Use the `Result` type for functions that can fail:

```typescript
type Result<T, E = string> = 
  | { data: T; error: null }
  | { data: null; error: E };

// Helper constructors
const ok = <T>(data: T): Result<T> => ({ data, error: null });
const err = <E = string>(error: E): Result<never, E> => ({ data: null, error });
```

### Usage Example

```typescript
async function fetchModel(modelId: string): Promise<Result<Model>> {
  try {
    const model = await db.models.findById(modelId);
    if (!model) {
      return err(`Model not found: ${modelId}`);
    }
    return ok(model);
  } catch (e) {
    return err(`Database error: ${String(e)}`);
  }
}

// Consuming the result
const result = await fetchModel(id);
if (result.error) {
  console.error(result.error);
  return;
}
const model = result.data; // TypeScript knows this is Model, not null
```

## API Error Responses

All API routes should return structured error responses:

```typescript
// Standard API error shape
interface APIError {
  error: string;
  details?: string;
  code?: number;
}

// In Next.js API routes
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const result = await someOperation();
  if (result.error) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: result.error } satisfies APIError);
  }
  return res.status(200).json(result.data);
}
```

## Error Logging

- Always log errors with sufficient context (request ID, user ID, operation name)
- Use structured logging — avoid bare `console.log` in production paths
- Do NOT log sensitive data: API keys, passwords, PII

```typescript
// Good
console.error(JSON.stringify({
  message: "Failed to process request",
  requestId,
  operation: "fetchCost",
  error: result.error,
}));

// Bad
console.log("error", e, apiKey); // leaks sensitive data
```

## Async Error Boundaries

Wrap top-level async operations in try/catch and convert to Result:

```typescript
async function safeAsync<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<Result<T>> {
  try {
    return ok(await fn());
  } catch (e) {
    return err(e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e));
  }
}
```

## Client-Side Error Handling

- Use React Error Boundaries for rendering errors
- Display user-friendly messages — never expose raw error strings to end users
- Log errors to an observability service (e.g., Sentry) in production

```typescript
// In components, map internal errors to user-facing messages
const userMessage = result.error?.includes("not found")
  ? "The requested resource could not be found."
  : "Something went wrong. Please try again.";
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- ❌ Swallowing errors silently (`catch (e) {}`)
- ❌ Throwing errors across async boundaries without catching
- ❌ Using `any` as an error type
- ❌ Returning `null` ambiguously (use `Result` instead)
- ❌ Deeply nested try/catch blocks — flatten with early returns
